          <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-white leading-[1.15] mb-6">
            How to Pre-Sort DSLR RAW Files for Siril Stacking in Under 60 Seconds
          </h1>

          <p class="text-lg sm:text-xl text-neutral-300 leading-relaxed font-normal">
            Siril's automated stacking scripts need four strict subdirectories: <code class="font-mono text-sm bg-black/40 text-[#00FFFF] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">/lights</code>, <code class="font-mono text-sm bg-black/40 text-[#00FFFF] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">/darks</code>, <code class="font-mono text-sm bg-black/40 text-[#00FFFF] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">/flats</code>, and <code class="font-mono text-sm bg-black/40 text-[#00FFFF] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">/biases</code>. Here is how to stop squinting at identical-looking black thumbnails at 2:00 AM and sort a full night's shoot in under a minute.
          </p>
        </header>

        <!-- Body -->
        <div class="text-neutral-300 leading-relaxed space-y-6 text-base font-normal">
          <p>
            It is 2:30 AM. You just dragged yourself inside from a freezing field, hands numb, tripod frozen, and your star tracker packed back into its case. You plug your SD card into your card reader, open your file manager, and stare at 450 uncompressed RAW files.
          </p>
          <p>
            Every single thumbnail looks like an identical square of pitch black.
          </p>
          <p>
            If your processing pipeline runs through <strong class="text-white">Siril</strong> (easily the best free, open-source astrophotography pre-processing software available today), you already know the bottleneck. Siril’s automated scripts (such as <code class="font-mono text-xs bg-black/40 text-[#00FFFF] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">OSC_Preprocessing</code> or <code class="font-mono text-xs bg-black/40 text-[#00FFFF] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">OSC_Preprocessing_WithoutFlat</code>) are incredible time-savers, but they require one strict, non-negotiable folder structure:
          </p>

          <!-- Code directory tree block -->
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-[#0b101b] p-5 font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm text-neutral-200 overflow-x-auto shadow-inner my-6">
            <div class="text-[#00FFFF] font-bold mb-2">Siril Expected Working Directory Structure:</div>
<pre class="leading-relaxed"><code>working_directory/
├── lights/   (Your deep sky exposures: e.g. 60s @ ISO 800)
├── darks/    (Same shutter speed &amp; ISO, lens cap ON)
├── flats/    (Evenly illuminated white panel: e.g. 1/20s @ ISO 800)
└── biases/   (Fastest possible shutter: e.g. 1/4000s @ ISO 800)</code></pre>
          </div>

          <p>
            If even one dark frame ends up inside your <code class="font-mono text-xs bg-black/40 text-[#00FFFF] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">/lights</code> folder, Siril's background normalization fails. If you accidentally put flats into biases, the entire master calibration stack is ruined.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="h-6 w-1 rounded-full bg-[#00FFFF]"></span>
            <span>Why Manual Sorting Fails at 2:00 AM</span>
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most DSLR astrophotographers sort files by clicking through Windows Explorer or Finder, sorting by "Date Modified," opening file properties to check shutter speeds, and dragging files into manually created folders.
          </p>
          <p>
            In practice, this method has three major vulnerabilities:
          </p>

          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 my-6">
            <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-[#101726] p-5">
              <div class="font-bold text-white text-base mb-1">1. Zero Visual Difference</div>
              <p class="text-neutral-400 text-sm">
                Unstretched astrophotography RAW files look like pure pitch black in file explorer thumbnails. A 60-second light frame looks identical to a 60-second dark frame or a 1/4000-second bias frame.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-[#101726] p-5">
              <div class="font-bold text-white text-base mb-1">2. Accidental Mixing Crashes Scripts</div>
              <p class="text-neutral-400 text-sm">
                If you shot multiple targets or interrupted your dark frame sequence, timestamps jump unpredictably. A single dark frame misplaced into lights ruins star alignment and causes calibration errors.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-[#101726] p-5">
              <div class="font-bold text-white text-base mb-1">3. Mental Fatigue &amp; Time Drain</div>
              <p class="text-neutral-400 text-sm">
                Manually selecting 300+ files and inspecting properties takes 15–30 minutes of tedious manual labor after hours of freezing out in the cold.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="h-6 w-1 rounded-full bg-[#00FFFF]"></span>
            <span>The Solution: Visual 2D Coordinate Clustering</span>
          </h2>

          <p>
            Instead of reading raw file names, think about how calibration frames actually behave in physics:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li><strong class="text-white">Flats:</strong> High frame luminance (sensor exposed to light panel), very short exposure.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Lights:</strong> Low-to-moderate luminance (dark night sky), long exposure duration (30s–180s).</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Darks:</strong> Zero light luminance (lens cap on), long exposure duration identical to lights.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Biases:</strong> Zero light luminance, fastest shutter speed possible (1/4000s or 1/8000s).</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            When you plot every file on a 2D coordinate space—where the <strong class="text-white">X-axis is Exposure Duration (seconds)</strong> and the <strong class="text-white">Y-axis is Mean Luminance (%)</strong>—your shoot naturally groups into four unmistakable, separated clusters:
          </p>

          <!-- Graph Screenshot Card -->
